WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.595
You've learned
about how RNNs work

2
00:00:02.595 --> 00:00:04.380
and how they can be applied

3
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.855
to problems like name
entity recognition

4
00:00:06.855 --> 00:00:09.525
as well as to language modeling.

5
00:00:09.525 --> 00:00:13.185
You saw how back propagation
can be used to train an RNN.

6
00:00:13.185 --> 00:00:15.390
It turns out that one
of the problems of

7
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.880
the basic RNN algorithm
is that it runs

8
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.595
into vanishing
gradient problems.

9
00:00:20.595 --> 00:00:23.640
Let's discuss that and in the
next few videos we'll talk

10
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.080
about some solutions that

11
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.165
will help to address
this problem.

12
00:00:27.165 --> 00:00:31.020
You've seen pictures of
RNNs that look like this.

13
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.500
Let's take a language
modeling example.

14
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.975
Let's say you see this sentence.

15
00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:42.415
The cat, which already

16
00:00:42.415 --> 00:00:43.955
ate and maybe already

17
00:00:43.955 --> 00:00:45.410
ate a bunch of food
that was delicious,

18
00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:50.070
dot dot, dot, dot, dot was full.

19
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.360
To be consistent is because
the cat is singular,

20
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.070
it should be the cat was
where there was the cats,

21
00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:00.320
which already ate a bunch

22
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.120
of food was delicious
and the apples and

23
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.675
pears and so on were full.

24
00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:14.130
To be consistent, it should
be cat was or cats were.

25
00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.150
This is one example of
when language can have

26
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.760
very long-term
dependencies where it

27
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.740
worded as much earlier can

28
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.910
affect what needs to come
much later in the sentence.

29
00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.830
But it turns out
that the basic RNN

30
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.300
we've seen so far is not

31
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.965
very good at capturing very
long-term dependencies.

32
00:01:32.965 --> 00:01:34.910
To explain why, you might

33
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.090
remember from our earlier
discussions of training

34
00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.710
very deep neural networks

35
00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.385
that we talked about the
vanishing gradients problem.

36
00:01:43.385 --> 00:01:45.290
This is a very, very
deep neural network,

37
00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.420
say 100 years or
even much deeper.

38
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.030
Then you would carry out forward

39
00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.090
prop from left to right
and then backprop.

40
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.005
We said that if this is a
very deep neural network,

41
00:01:57.005 --> 00:01:59.060
then the gradient
from this output

42
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.160
y would have a very
hard time propagating

43
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.225
back to affect the weights
of these earlier layers,

44
00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:07.580
to affect the computations
of the earlier layers.

45
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.220
For an RNN with a
similar problem,

46
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.610
you have forward prop going
from left to right and

47
00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.985
then backprop going
from right to left.

48
00:02:17.985 --> 00:02:20.870
It can be quite
difficult because of

49
00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.440
the same vanishing gradients
problem for the outputs of

50
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.070
the errors associated with
the later timesteps to

51
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.465
affect the computations
that are earlier.

52
00:02:32.465 --> 00:02:34.160
In practice, what this means

53
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.630
is it might be difficult to get

54
00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.779
a neural network to realize
that it needs to memorize.

55
00:02:38.779 --> 00:02:40.460
Did you see a singular noun or

56
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.050
a plural noun so that later

57
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.410
on in the sequence it can
generate either was or were,

58
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.055
depending on whether it
was singular or plural.

59
00:02:49.055 --> 00:02:51.260
Notice that in
English this stuff in

60
00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.630
the middle could be
arbitrarily long.

61
00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:57.740
You might need to memorize
the singular plural for

62
00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.510
a very long time before you get

63
00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:03.210
to use that bit of information.

64
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.375
Because of this problem,

65
00:03:05.375 --> 00:03:09.440
the basic RNN model has
many local influences,

66
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.980
meaning that the output y hat

67
00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.425
three is mainly influenced by

68
00:03:15.425 --> 00:03:19.295
values close to y hat three and

69
00:03:19.295 --> 00:03:21.350
a value here is
mainly influenced

70
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.240
by inputs that are
somewhat close.

71
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.330
It's difficult for the
output here to be strongly

72
00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:27.980
influenced by an input that

73
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.080
was very early in the sequence.

74
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.585
This is because
whatever the output is,

75
00:03:32.585 --> 00:03:34.400
whether this got it
right, this got it wrong,

76
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.785
it's just very difficult
for the error to

77
00:03:36.785 --> 00:03:38.120
backpropagate all the way

78
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.665
to the beginning
of the sequence,

79
00:03:39.665 --> 00:03:42.020
and therefore to modify how

80
00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.190
the neural network is doing

81
00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.755
computations earlier
in the sequence.

82
00:03:45.755 --> 00:03:49.715
This is a weakness of
the basic RNN algorithm,

83
00:03:49.715 --> 00:03:53.585
one which will to address
in the next few videos.

84
00:03:53.585 --> 00:03:55.130
But if we don't address it,

85
00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.770
then RNNs tend not to be

86
00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.070
very good at capturing
long-range dependencies.

87
00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.400
Even though this
discussion has focused

88
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.145
on vanishing gradients,
you remember,

89
00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:08.620
when we're talking about very
deep neural networks that

90
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.140
we also talked about
exploding gradients.

91
00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.355
Where doing backprop,

92
00:04:12.355 --> 00:04:13.600
the ingredients should not

93
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.190
just decrease
exponentially they may

94
00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:16.990
also increase exponentially with

95
00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.505
the number of layers
you go through.

96
00:04:18.505 --> 00:04:20.950
It turns out that
vanishing gradients

97
00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.745
tends to be the biggest
problem with training RNNs.

98
00:04:23.745 --> 00:04:25.570
Although when
exploding gradients

99
00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.850
happens it can be
catastrophic because

100
00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.560
the exponentially
large gradients

101
00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.900
can cause your
parameters to become

102
00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.960
so large that your neural
network parameters

103
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.475
get really messed up.

104
00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:39.130
It turns out that exploding
gradients are easier

105
00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.660
to spot because the
parameter has just blow up.

106
00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.110
You might often see
NaNs, not a numbers,

107
00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:48.905
meaning results of a
numerical overflow

108
00:04:48.905 --> 00:04:51.205
in your neural
network computation.

109
00:04:51.205 --> 00:04:53.180
If you do see
exploding gradients,

110
00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.200
one solution to that is
apply gradients clipping.

111
00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.690
All that means is,

112
00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:01.595
look at your gradient vectors,

113
00:05:01.595 --> 00:05:06.230
and if it is bigger
than some threshold,

114
00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.210
re-scale some of your
gradient vectors

115
00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:09.410
so that it's not too big,

116
00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.935
so that is clipped according
to some maximum value.

117
00:05:12.935 --> 00:05:15.454
If you see exploding gradients,

118
00:05:15.454 --> 00:05:18.815
if your derivatives do
explore the resilience,

119
00:05:18.815 --> 00:05:20.615
just apply gradient clipping.

120
00:05:20.615 --> 00:05:23.960
That's a relatively
robust solution

121
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.105
that will take care of
exploding gradients.

122
00:05:26.105 --> 00:05:28.970
But vanishing gradients
is much harder to

123
00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:33.265
solve and it will be the
subject of the next few videos.

124
00:05:33.265 --> 00:05:35.900
To summarize, in
an earlier course,

125
00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.675
you saw how we're training
a very deep neural network.

126
00:05:38.675 --> 00:05:41.270
You can run into vanishing
gradient or exploding

127
00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.560
gradient problems where the

128
00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.840
derivative either
decreases exponentially,

129
00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.730
or grows exponentially as

130
00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.965
a function of the
number of layers.

131
00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:51.230
An RNN, say an RNN

132
00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.750
processing data over
1,000 times sets,

133
00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.220
or over 10,000 times sets,

134
00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.470
that's basically
a 1,000 layer or

135
00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.885
like a 10,000 layer
neural network.

136
00:05:59.885 --> 00:06:03.300
It too runs into these
types of problems.

137
00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.640
Exploding gradients
you could solve

138
00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.090
address by just using
gradient clipping,

139
00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.300
but vanishing gradients will
take way more to address.

140
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:14.120
What we'll do in the next
video is talk about GRUs,

141
00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.560
a greater recurrent units,

142
00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:18.425
which is a very effective
solution for addressing

143
00:06:18.425 --> 00:06:20.900
the vanishing gradient
problem and will allow

144
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.490
your neural network to capture

145
00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:24.950
much longer range dependencies.

146
00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:27.570
Let's go on to the next video.